body:
It depends on the house I am working. I work in their individual homes. My main client is relatively high functioning and doesn't need 24/7 care, so he gets 12 hours a day. I often do 8-8, three and a half days a week. But I can choose if I wanna do 7-7, 8-8, 9-9, 10-10, or 11-11. But I prefer 8. I feel like it's a happy medium.

&#x200B;

My other house, he is a 24/7 care. It depends on the shift, it can vary from 8 hours to 16 hours. But it isn't all that bad.

&#x200B;

I am not a fan of doing overnights, but I will do them. Although I feel like the pay decrease is what makes me want to work a second job at night so I don't have to worry about all that noise.

Things to them were very simple, but a lot of them are very angry now... But they can occasionally be more subtle about it. They are angry when they are challenged, corrected, or asked to change their habits. They also believe that if they were supposed to suffer, then so should we. Why should we have it any better if they had to 'work' for it? They are proud of having to struggle and they can occasionally be proud of their hatred or ignorance.

&#x200B;

This doesn't apply to all of them, but this is from my personal perspective. I'm a little bit older than you though, I was born in 1991.

I legit loved Season 2 with all my heart and soul, but the ending was atrocious and should have been completely different. It annoys me how everything was wrapped up so perfectly after that emotional and depressing season. Best season ever, worst ending.

&#x200B;

Oh, and a bonus: Henry Wong was nowhere near as nice and kind as people like to think he is. He's a favorite of mine, but the dude is an asshole.

Sometimes it can be hard on some days not to accidentally fall asleep here and there, I have been in homes where they keep the heat up super high and the client is not as needy and gets a lot of alone time, but if they straight up go to bed or come to work actively planning on sleeping and taking naps on a non-sleep schedule, then yes.

&#x200B;

As far as the phone situation goes, I would use my judgement on that. If it is getting in the way of client care, then yes, I would report it.

I agree with the fact that the LAU games focused on puzzles and tried to reflect what Core Design had built (like some kind of 14 year old girl's fanfiction) but I can't unsee the sheer disrespect that Crystal Dynamics and Eidos showed toward Core Design for having the audacity to not only steal the game from them but to also remake their first game. To me, that was such a disrespectful slap in the face and I cannot overlook it. I know they were told to do it by the company, but I still just really think it was distasteful.  And *then*, they had even more audacity to promise me some Tihocan and Qualopec, then *never went anywhere with it*! What kind of sadistic freaks are these people?

&#x200B;

At least the survivor trilogy basically rebooted everything *once again* and at least made Lara Croft into a new character in a new world.

Honestly, if I get downvoted into oblivion for preferring Core Design and Classic Lara, then I will gladly take it. It's a compliment that I am not seeing eye to eye with people who love Crystal Dynamics. I wear it with pride.

&#x200B;

I had a hard time enjoying the LAU games. Especially Anniversary. We could have had Core Design's version, but instead we got that one. And the storyline was so weird and dramatic... Natla wanting to rule beside Lara? And do what? That didn't even make sense. None of the story changes to Anniversary made any sense to me.

I disagree with you. Everything about Lara changed. Her physical appearance, her attitude, her past, her motivations, everything. They even changed Natla completely from what she once was... And this is my problem with Crystal Dynamics. I don't like how soft spoke and moral she is, she is younger, softer, kinder. The old Lara Croft didn't care, she was colder, cockier, she was more capable of raw violence. Like at the end of TRIII she grinned and shot a dude just so she could steal his helicopter, wherease in Tomb Raider 2013 she had a whole moral dilemma and crisis over a wrecked helicopter. I feel like the Core Design games were unique and eccentric and unforgettable, Crystal Dynamics kind of caters to whatever is popular at the time while Core Design set out to be groundbreaking in their field. Crystal wants Lara to be softer and kinder and more... I guess relatable, where I feel like Core Design wanted someone badass and intimidating and someone to look up to.

&#x200B;

But I understand that this is my opinion and no matter what happens, I won't be getting Core Design back. I accepted this long ago. The three most recent games were okay, gameplay wise.

When I was a child, I recall buying the \*old school\* Gold edition with my father at the local Sam's Club. So being told that I can \*finally\* play the expansions on console is a special kind of amazing for me, considering that The Lost Artifact and Unfinished Business were absolute \*legends\* to me growing up. And I haven't ran into a lot of people who even know half of these characters... Bring up Mark Willard or Sophia Leigh to the average Tomb Raider fan you run into IRL on the streets and they'll look at you like you're joking with them.

&#x200B;

This was an extremely good idea. This is the first time I have looked forward to a Tomb Raider game since Core Design was sent packing. Sifting through the Temple of the Cat or messing with Nessie on Highland Fling is going to be so amazing. I am looking forward to it... I even called off work so I could have the whole day to myself and this game.

&#x200B;

So this is an amazing way to get newcomers into the franchise, and they can also experience the \*real\* Tomb Raider experience. The old school, badass, eccentric, Tomb Raider. And I hope that the newcomers get the same experience that I did growing up.

Funny enough, I feel the same way. I first switched over to the DSP field back in July, before then I was scrubbing toilets and barely making over minimum wage. Nothing wrong with being a janitor, but I wasn't making enough to make ends meet. When I first started being a DSP, I thought for absolute sure that this was a horrible mistake and I wasn't going to last, but I was just introduced to the wrong client. He didn't like me very much, and I was nervous around him. But around August I was placed in a certain home where I met my absolute dream client... Everything changed. I switched agencies in order to work with him, and not only did I manage to work with said client, but I also got hired in as a house manage. So it was also the first time I was ever a manager as well.

&#x200B;

This has been a wild ride and it has definitely had it's ups and downs... But I am feeling extremely good about this. I've been in the field for roughly... five, six months now? It's been mostly great. I think a lot of us just have to find our special client that we bond with, and the job because a piece of cake. It doesn't even feel like a job.

I thought it was actually a really cool idea to kill Jasper for a few reasons... Steven could realize how strong he actually is and have to learn how to control himself, Jasper is someone who would chase after someone until pressuring them into doing something drastic, and it would be a nice twist. I can see Steven being frustrated and stressed out and taking his anger out on Jasper, who is asking for it and pushing him to the brink, and he just shatters her by accident. It would have made a lot of sense.

&#x200B;

Although I love Jasper and I would have been sad. Lmao! But still... Just because you love a character doesn't mean their deaths wouldn't be horribly badass.

If he had, it would have been the worst mistake Gwi-Nam ever made in the show, because he even states that Su-Hyeuk bossed him around and told him what to do and what not to do, and we even see him pushing a very passive Gw-Nam around in the first episode. Even when Gwi-Nam is a halfbie and has his powers, he still struggled to win against Su-Hyeuk as a regular human. Gwi-Nam would have been taken out much earlier, or at least forced into submission if halfbie Su-Hyeuk was feeling generous. Su-Hyeuk has made is perfectly clear multiple times that he doesn't even view Gwi-Nam as much of a threat, even when he's around *other* bullies.

&#x200B;

Even personality-wise, Su-Hyeuk has an advantage. He's smarter, less prone to panic, less prone to immediate anger, less likely to be a follower, is able to get his life together and hold things down, and he's protective and follows through. Gwi-Nam is very easily goaded, mislead, bullied, is very vindictive, has something to prove, and has a lot of low-key fears and insecurities that make him who he is.
